  rather than the id alone. The reason is specific to having no backend: if a later form wants to show "signed in as …",
  a userId alone can't be resolved into a name, because there's nothing to ask. The username is already in hand at
  login, so carrying it costs one field. Trivial to drop if you'd rather store strictly the id.

  [1m[38;5;153mlogin()[39m's new contract.[0m It stops being given a userId and starts returning one:

  [34mfunction[39m [33mlogin[39m(username, password) {
    [32m// Stub: would POST to API_ENDPOINT in real app[39m
    [34mreturn[39m { [36msuccess[39m: [34mtrue[39m, [36muserId[39m: STUB_USER_ID, username };
  }

  Two deliberate choices there. The stub userId is a [1mnamed constant with an obviously-fake value[0m, not a generated one —
  a stub that mints plausible-looking ids invites someone to treat them as real identity later. And the [38;5;153muser[39m field is
  renamed to [38;5;153musername[39m for consistency with the stored record; that's safe because [38;5;153mapp.js:23[39m is the only caller in the
  repo.

  [1m[38;5;153mlogin()[39m does not write to storage.[0m The submit handler does:

  [34mconst[39m result = [33mlogin[39m(username, password);
  [34mif[39m (result.success) [33msetSession[39m({ [36muserId[39m: result.userId, [36musername[39m: result.username });

  Keeping the write in the caller means [38;5;153mlogin()[39m stays a pure stand-in for a network call — testable, and honest about
  the fact that it will become async once [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m is real. If persistence were hidden inside it, every future test
  of login would have storage side effects.

  [1mLoading.[0m [38;5;153mindex.html[39m becomes [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js">[39m, and [38;5;153mapp.js[39m gains [38;5;153mimport { setSession, clearSession }
[39m  [38;5;153mfrom "./session.js";[39m. Later forms import from the same file; no script-tag ordering to remember.

  [1mLogout.[0m [38;5;153mindex.html[39m gets a logout button wired to [38;5;153mclearSession()[39m. I'm proposing it always visible rather than toggling
  on session state — showing/hiding it means introducing UI state management, which is a bigger idea than this design
  should smuggle in.

  Does that look right so far?

